[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: FRRouting control plane on router1, specifically the RIP daemon and the frr/watchfrr service.

Mechanism: With RIP down on router1, no dynamic routes are exchanged across its core links (10.16.1.0/24 to router0 and 10.20.1.0/24 to router2). Forwarding still works for directly connected and a couple of static kernel routes, so some zones using alternate paths can still talk, but prefixes that relied on router1 to learn/propagate routes are no longer reachable end-to-end. This explains why hosts can reach their local gateways and some inter-zone traffic works, while others (for example 172.16.10.x ↔ 172.18.51.x) fail.

Localization and evidence: On router1, the FRR service is failed (systemctl frr: Active: failed). Only zebra is running (ps shows /usr/lib/frr/zebra; no ripd/watchfrr processes). vtysh shows no running config, and the routing table has only connected and kernel entries (show ip route: C>* 10.16.1.0/24, C>* 10.20.1.0/24, K>* 172.18.50/51 via 10.20.1.2), with no R routes. Meanwhile, cross-zone reachability tests show partial failures (e.g., pc4→pc15 times out), consistent with loss of dynamic routing through router1.

Root cause: A recent change on router1 stopped the FRR service, leaving ripd down and halting RIP on both core interfaces.

Corrective fix: Restore routing by restarting and enabling FRR on router1 (ensure watchfrr and ripd run), confirm ripd is enabled in /etc/frr/daemons, then verify neighbors and routes (show ip rip, show ip route should show R>* entries on 10.16.1.0/24 and 10.20.1.0/24). Review logs to prevent recurrence. === END ===